/*
 * HW MRADIX-accelerated TopK kernel for GCU400 via TLE-RAW, called once per row
 * by a TLE-Struct launcher (topk_mradix.py).
 *
 * Ported from the Enflame kurama tree, where it is developed and validated as
 * triton_gcu/test/triton36/python/triton/gcu_tle/raw/tops/
 * 04-topk-mradix-struct-raw-persistent.{py,tops}. Keep the two copies in sync:
 * kurama is the upstream source of truth for this kernel.
 *
 * Two stages were moved across the Struct/Raw boundary, everything else is
 * left alone:
 *     S1's data movement (the row copy)  -> tle.gpu.copy in the launcher
 *     S5 (the k-element writeback)       -> local_ptr + tl.store in the launcher
 * The launcher also owns the launch geometry and the persistent row loop, so
 * this region has no row loop, no DTE channel and no event of its own. What is
 * left here is the row's compute: convert in place, seven MRADIX passes, the
 * reclaim scatter, and the staging the launcher reads back.
 *
 * Execution geometry:
 *   - One hw thread per SIP with 4 subthreads each (num_warps = 4; 1 does not
 *     work -- the reclaim barriers and the staging split assume 4 subthreads).
 *     MRADIX is a shared per-SIP unit, so only one hw thread per SIP may drive
 *     it.
 *   - Persistent kernel on the Struct side: grid = min(SIP_COUNT, M) blocks,
 *     blockDim 1, so one block is one hw thread (grid == gbl_thr_num = 24).
 *     The launcher walks the rows of this hw thread with
 *     row = row_base + r * row_stride -- the same GetThreadIdx()/GetThreadNum()
 *     split topsop uses (radix_batch_split.h:1014-1035) -- stages row r into
 *     l1_buf and calls this entry once per row. The mapping therefore stays in
 *     Python, which is what lets a different distribution be tried without
 *     touching this file.
 *   - Capacity: N <= MAX_L1_CACHE (65536 fp32) and K <= WS_STAGE_K_MAX (640).
 *     The SIP's VDMEM is 896KB (229376 fp32); the launcher allocates the row
 *     buffer (BLOCK_N = max(128, next_pow2(N)) words, i.e. <= 256KB) plus this
 *     file's 16KB reclaim workspace / staging arena, so <= 272KB are in use,
 *     and passes both in as plain pointers (see OWNERSHIP at the entry). 64K is
 *     the largest row the L1 radix-select is competitive on.
 *     The host side (topk_mradix.py: L1_MAX_COLS /
 *     TOPK_K_MAX) enforces the same envelope, so an oversized call fails loudly
 *     instead of silently overflowing VDMEM.
 *
 * Algorithm (radix-SELECT, plus a bitonic argsort of the k winners):
 *   The row arrives in l1_buf and is converted in place to order-preserving
 *   sort keys -- 32-bit keys for a fp32 row, 16-bit keys for a fp16/bf16 row
 *   (in_kind, see the 16-bit note below). Up to seven MRADIX passes (5-bit
 *   digits, MSB first; the top digit only carries 2 bits of a fp32 key, 1 bit
 *   of a 16-bit one) narrow the search to the k-th
 *   largest key: every pass rescans the full row (the candidate set is never
 *   compacted), and the 32-bin walk stops early when a bin holds exactly the
 *   remaining candidates -- its lower bound is then the threshold and still
 *   selects exactly K lanes.
 *   The reclaim pass scatters those lanes into staging (one ge stream when it
 *   holds exactly K lanes, otherwise gt lanes first and then eq truncated at
 *   K). Reclaim order is unspecified, so it alone satisfies
 *   torch.topk(sorted=False); when the caller asks for sorted=True, S4 then
 *   argsorts those k (key, index) pairs in place -- descending key, ties by
 *   ascending row position -- and the launcher copies staging[0, K) out either
 *   way.
 *   Stage banners in the kernel (stage, and the subthreads that run it):
 *     S1  vt2ut           l1_buf row -> sort-uint keys, in place (all
 *                         subthreads; the row itself was staged by tle.gpu.copy)
 *     S2  histogram       digit passes -> k-th key (sub0 only)
 *     S3  reclaim         top-k lanes -> staging (count: all; prefix: sub0
 *                         plus the table: sub2; scatter: all)
 *     S4  sort            staging[0, width) argsort in place (sub0 only,
 *                         sorted=True calls only; width = next_pow2(K) rounded
 *                         up to VEC_LEN, so K <= 512)
 *     S5  writeback       not here: the launcher reads staging[0, K) through
 *                         tle.gpu.local_ptr and stores it to the output row
 *                         (sorted output when is_sorted was set)
 *   So S1's copy and S5 are the two stages on the Struct side; the boundary
 *   runs between "the row is in l1_buf" and "the top-k lanes are in staging",
 *   and nothing inside that interval knows how either end is transported.
 *
 * Scheduling and cost model (gcu400, topsprof device time, median n=80):
 *   The scheduling unit is a whole row: one hw thread per SIP, rows walked
 *   serially, so T_total ~= T_launch(~3.0us) + ceil(M / 24) * T_row(N, K)
 *   and NOT (M / 24) * T_row: M is rounded up to the 24-SIP wave and the last
 *   wave runs partly idle (wave quantization). At N=32768, K=256:
 *       M=120 (5 waves)  113.0us
 *       M=128 (6 waves)  133.1us   <- ~90% SIP utilization
 *       M=144 (6 waves)  135.1us
 *   so M=128 already pays for 6 full waves while doing 5.3 waves of work:
 *   ~10% of that shape is tail. (The last wave is cheaper than a full one --
 *   133.1 vs 135.1 -- because only 8 of 24 SIPs stay busy.)
 *   Small N is row/launch bound: a row costs ~5us whatever N is until the
 *   histogram starts to dominate. Large N is histogram-throughput bound: seven
 *   passes over N/VEC_LEN chunks plus the 32-bin scalar bin walk, with K
 *   adding ~0.017us per non-empty chunk (comp_cnt ~ K) of reclaim scatter.
 *   Reducing the tail needs a unit finer than a row, i.e. r SIPs sharing one
 *   row: cut the row into r bands, histogram per band, merge the 32-bin
 *   partials behind a barrier, then reclaim inside each band (histograms
 *   merge, the candidates never do), which also raises the reachable N by r.
 *   That shape is not used here: the per-digit cross-SIP barrier plus the
 *   shared-histogram round trip through global memory cost more than the ~10%
 *   it wins, and with BLOCK_SIZE=1 a block is one hw thread, so r > 1 needs a
 *   grid-scope barrier over all 24 SIPs. A block-scope version (6 hw threads
 *   per block, grid = 4) is an untried variant.
 *   Subthreads do not partition a row: all subthreads of a SIP share its radix
 *   engine, so 2 groups of 4 (8 subthreads) only keeps two whole rows in
 *   flight and ping-pongs the engine between them. That makes the unit coarser
 *   (ceil(M/48) waves, still ~90% at M=128) and halves the VDMEM budget per
 *   row; it does not touch the tail.
 *   What the boundary costs: the row copy is now a Struct op whose consumer
 *   (this region) is a separate llvm.call, so the copy and the compute do not
 *   overlap -- the DTE wait lands on the call's input. Measured at
 *   128x32768x256 with do_bench (interleaved samples, medians): 140.2us for
 *   topk_mradix_l1.tops, which prefetches the next row under the current one,
 *   versus 154.2us here. The ~14us is the row copy itself (~2.5us/row,
 *   52GB/s) going serial, not a slower compute: the raw region's own time is
 *   the same as the standalone kernel's. Recovering it needs the copy and the
 *   compute to overlap again, which is a scheduling decision, not an algorithm
 *   one -- see the S1 banner.
 *
 * Toolchain constraints (topscc v4.0.11):
 *   - tcle::set_vl MUST be set at kernel entry: without it the
 *     MRADIX/DTE machinery runs with a wrong vector length and any MRADIX use
 *     after a DTE slice faults the SIP at runtime. It must also be the lane
 *     width of the vectors the row is scanned with (VEC_LEN16 = 256 for a
 *     16-bit row, VEC_LEN = 128 for fp32): popc_mb/miota/cvt read a mask back
 *     at min(mask width, VL), so a too-small VL truncates the 256-lane ops.
 *   - tcle::leaptr/TAR auto-incrementing loads work through TLE-RAW's MLIR
 *     round-trip: tcle::simple_leaptr<VUI> lowers to l.tvldoa.w through the
 *     full GCU converter chain, so the histogram passes load without any
 *     per-load address arithmetic, and the batched 16x l.tvldoa.w costs
 *     ~3 cyc/vector against 7-9 for the serial l.vldoa + address loop.
 *   - The topscc function attributes (enable_software_pipeliner,
 *     no_mem_alias_in_vldst_tar, loop_iterator_less_than_1024,
 *     enable_bc_resolver) written as function __attribute__ survive the
 *     tle_raw region as LLVM function attrs (e.g. attributes #2 in the
 *     topscc -emit-llvm output) and shrink the compiled kernel .text. They
 *     are applied to the histogram helper and to the kernel entry.
 *   - Hot loops are unrolled with `#pragma clang loop unroll(full)`. That
 *     needs the optimizer, and the TLE-Raw `.tops` is always built at -O3
 *     (kTopsccOptLevel in gcu_compiler/lib/Target/LLVM/GCU/Target.cpp), so
 *     the pragma is never dropped.
 *
 * Limits (not implemented):
 *   - split-row multi-SIP cooperation (r SIPs per row, see the cost model)
 *   - 2 subthread groups sharing the radix engine (two rows in flight per SIP)
 *   - overlapping the launcher's row copy with this region's compute
 *   - find_kth shrink/compaction for the top-K prefix: later digit passes
 *     rescan the whole row instead of narrowing to the kept bin
 *   - uint8 inputs, fp8 inputs
 *   - topk_per_row_prefill/decode/persistent modes
 *
 * 16-bit keys (fp16/bf16 rows, in_kind != 0):
 *   A fp16/bf16 value's 16-bit pattern is order-isomorphic to the value, so the
 *   row is staged raw (2B per element, a plain tle.gpu.copy in the launcher)
 *   and VT2UT16 folds it to 16-bit keys in place. Scanning 16-bit keys is the
 *   point: half the S1/S2 traffic, and every band-mask op covers 256 elements
 *   per register instead of 128. The digit walk then starts at digit 3 (bit
 *   15) instead of digit 6, i.e. four passes instead of seven for a fp32 row
 *   (both 16-bit dtypes use the same four: unlike a fp32 key, a 16-bit key has
 *   no digits that are constant for the dtype). MRADIX counts 32-bit lanes, so
 *   each u16 vector is widened once per pass with a cvt into two VUI halves --
 *   the count cost per element is the same as the fp32 path, the traffic and
 *   the mask ops are not.
 *   `desired` stays a plain key value (find_k_bin works on the 32-bit digit
 *   grid), so the reclaim compares zero-extended u16 keys against it and only
 *   has to rebuild the fp32 value for the scattered lanes: bf16 is one
 *   pattern restore + shift, fp16 one pattern restore + one hardware cvt. Staging therefore
 *   still holds fp32 words, and S4/S5 do not know about dtypes at all.
 */

#include <stdint.h>
#include <tcle_general.h>
#include <tops.h>
#include <tops/topscc_types.h>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <functional>
#include <utility>

typedef __vector unsigned int VUI;
typedef __vector int          VSI;
typedef __vector bool int     VMASK;

/* 16-bit key domain (fp16/bf16 rows). A vector register is 512B, so one u16
   vector holds VEC_LEN16 = 256 keys while a u32 vector holds VEC_LEN = 128:
   the whole point of the narrow domain is that a scan reads 2B per element
   and every masking op covers twice as many elements. Widening to u32 (which
   the MRADIX unit requires, it counts 32-bit lanes) happens per vector with
   one cvt into __vector2 unsigned int, i.e. two VUI halves. */
typedef __vector unsigned short VUS;
typedef __vector float          VF;
typedef __vector2 unsigned int  VUI2;   /* 2 x VEC_LEN u32 lanes */
typedef __vector2 float         VF2;    /* 2 x VEC_LEN f32 lanes */
typedef __vector __fp16         VF16;   /* VEC_LEN16 half lanes */

constexpr int DSA_BITS      = 5;
constexpr int NUM_BINS      = 1 << DSA_BITS;
constexpr int VEC_LEN       = 128;
constexpr int VEC_LEN16     = 2 * VEC_LEN; /* 256: u16 lanes per register */
constexpr int TYPE_BITS     = 32;
constexpr int MAX_SHIFT_NUM = (TYPE_BITS + DSA_BITS - 1) / DSA_BITS; /* = 7 */
/* A 16-bit key occupies the low 4 digits of the 32-bit digit grid, so the
   digit walk starts at digit 3 (bit 15) instead of digit 6 (bit 30) and the
   key never leaves the low half. find_k_bin still works on the 32-bit grid
   (MAX_SHIFT_NUM), which is what keeps `desired` a plain key value. */
constexpr int TYPE_BITS16   = 16;
constexpr int SHIFT_NUM16   = (TYPE_BITS16 + DSA_BITS - 1) / DSA_BITS; /* 4 */
constexpr int FIRST_DIGIT16 = MAX_SHIFT_NUM - SHIFT_NUM16;             /* 3 */
constexpr uint32_t PAD_VALUE_DESC = 0u;

// Radix-select caches the row's sort-uint keys in l1_buf (MAX_L1_CACHE).
// MAX_L1_CACHE = 65536 fp32 = 256KB of the SIP's 896KB VDMEM.
constexpr int MAX_L1_CACHE = 65536;

/* Reclaim workspace (reclaim_ws[WS_SIZE] uint32_t).  Two count streams are
   kept because the output is "all strictly-greater lanes, then the first
   (K - n_gt) equal lanes":
     [ge_count  fc_al) [ge_prefix fc_al) [gt_count fc_al) [gt_prefix fc_al)
     [valid_vec_idx fc_al) | pad | [stage_v K_MAX) [stage_i K_MAX) [bcast 3)
   fc_al = ALIGN_UP(full_chunks, VEC_LEN) with full_chunks = ALIGN_UP(N,
   VEC_LEN) / VEC_LEN. WS_FC_AL_MAX is the largest fc_al the supported N can
   produce (N = 65536 -> 512), so the count/prefix region can never run into the
   staging area; static_assert below pins that.  The staging area holds K slots
   per stream (K <= WS_STAGE_K_MAX), which is sufficient by construction: the gt
   stream has n_gt <= K-1 lanes and the eq stream is truncated at K - n_gt.

   Why WS_STAGE_K_MAX is 640 (the widest K this layout can carry):
     - stage_v starts at WS_STAGE_OFF = 2560 words;
     - the top 3 words (ws[4093..4095]) carry the values subthreads publish
       to each other (desired, comp_cnt, n_ge, n_gt) and are never part of
       staging, so staging must stop below them:
       2*K <= 4096 - 2560 - 3 = 1533 -> K <= 766;
     - stage_i must start 512B-aligned because the launcher reads both streams
       as whole VEC_LEN-lane tiles, so WS_STAGE_K_MAX must be a multiple of
       VEC_LEN words -> 640. The runtime K itself is free (it is only bounded
       by WS_STAGE_K_MAX).
   Going wider needs a bigger ws (host L1_WS_ELEMS / WS_SIZE here; this SIP
   has ~368KB of VDMEM free) or relocating the 3 publish words; see the host
   note at TOPK_K_MAX in topk_mradix.py. */
constexpr int WS_SIZE        = 4096;
constexpr int WS_FC_AL_MAX   = 512;
constexpr int WS_BCAST_SLOTS = 3;                /* ws[4093..4095] */
constexpr int WS_STAGE_K_MAX = 640;              /* K slots per stream */
constexpr int WS_STAGE_OFF   = 5 * WS_FC_AL_MAX; /* 2560: stage_v */
constexpr int WS_STAGE_I_OFF = WS_STAGE_OFF + WS_STAGE_K_MAX; /* 3200 */
static_assert(WS_STAGE_I_OFF + WS_STAGE_K_MAX + WS_BCAST_SLOTS <= WS_SIZE,
              "reclaim workspace too small for the staging area");
static_assert(WS_STAGE_OFF == 2560 && WS_STAGE_K_MAX == 640,
              "the launcher's WS_STAGE_OFF / WS_STAGE_K_MAX mirror these two");

/* Pad lanes of the staged row: the launcher's tle.gpu.copy pads [N, BLOCK_N)
 * with -inf (other=float("-inf")), which is 0xff800000, i.e.
 * __builtin_bit_cast(float, 0xff800000u). Under VT2UT it maps to 0x007fffff,
 * strictly below every finite key's image (values >= 0.0f map to
 * [0x80000000, 0xff800000)), so pad lanes can never enter a selected bin for
 * K <= N. Nothing below depends on HOW the row arrived, only on that value.
 * A 16-bit row pads with the same -inf: its pattern is 0xfc00 (fp16) /
 * 0xff80 (bf16), which VT2UT16 folds to 0x03ff / 0x007f, also strictly below
 * every finite 16-bit key, so the same argument holds per key width. */

using CmpGe = std::greater_equal<>;
using CmpGt = std::greater<>;
using CmpEq = std::equal_to<>;

static __device__ __forceinline__ VUI VT2UT(VUI u) {
  return u ^ (((VUI)0 - (u >> 31)) | (VUI)(0x80000000u));
}

static __device__ __forceinline__ VUI UT2VT(VUI u) {
  return u ^ (((u >> 31) - (VUI)1) | (VUI)(0x80000000u));
}

/* Same order-preserving sign-magnitude fold as VT2UT/UT2VT, one 16-bit key per
   lane: negatives (sign bit set) get bit 15 flipped into range and the
   remaining magnitude inverted, non-negatives just get the sign bit set. The
   arithmetic is done in 16-bit lanes (0 - 1 wraps to 0xFFFF), which is exactly
   VT2UT16/UT2VT16 in topsop's naming (compare_accumulate_gcu400.h:2118). */
static __device__ __forceinline__ VUS VT2UT16(VUS u) {
  return u ^ (((VUS)0 - (u >> 15)) | (VUS)(0x8000u));
}

static __device__ __forceinline__ VUS UT2VT16(VUS u) {
  return u ^ (((u >> 15) - (VUS)1) | (VUS)(0x8000u));
}

/* Rebuild the fp32 bit pattern of the 16-bit value a key came from. Only S3
   needs it (the staged values must stay fp32 words for S4 and the launcher's
   S5), and only for the lanes of a scattered chunk, so the cost is sparse.
     bf16  value bits are the pattern << 16. The key must be folded back to the
           pattern first (UT2VT16): the tempting "key16 << 16 already is the
           fp32 key" shortcut is wrong. A negative bf16 p has key16 = ~p, so
           key16 << 16 has its low 16 bits clear, while the real fp32 key
           ~(p << 16) has them set; UT2VT would then bake that 0xFFFF into the
           mantissa and every negative value came back one ULP too negative.
     fp16  needs the pattern back and a hardware cvt (exact for subnormals,
           which an arithmetic rebuild would get wrong). */
static __device__ __forceinline__ VUI key16_to_bf16_bits(VUI key_u32) {
  VUI k = key_u32 & (VUI)(0xFFFFu);
  /* 16-bit UT2VT16 in 32-bit lanes: bit 15 set -> only its sign bit flips,
     bit 15 clear -> the whole 16-bit magnitude inverts. Masking to 16 bits
     after the xor keeps the high half clear for the << 16 below. */
  VUI p = (k ^ (((k >> 15) - (VUI)(1u)) | (VUI)(0x8000u))) & (VUI)(0xFFFFu);
  return p << 16;
}

/* ---- 16-bit chunk access ------------------------------------------------
   S1 converts a fp16/bf16 row in place to 16-bit keys, so chunk c of 128 keys
   is one half of the VEC_LEN16-lane vector at (c / 2). These helpers hand a
   chunk to the code that expects VUI lanes:
     keys  the 128 keys zero-extended to u32. Order is preserved and
           find_k_bin leaves `desired` as a key value, so `keys >= vkey`
           compares in this domain for both 16-bit dtypes.
     k16   the whole 256-lane pair, only used for the fp16 value rebuild.
   A pair that straddles the end of N_padded still lies inside the staged
   window: the launcher pads [N, BLOCK_N) and BLOCK_N >= ALIGN_UP(N, 256). */
static __device__ __forceinline__ VUS load_pair16(const uint16_t *su16, int c) {
  return reinterpret_cast<const VUS *>(su16)[c >> 1];
}

static __device__ __forceinline__ VUI chunk_keys16(VUI2 pair, int c) {
  return (c & 1) ? tcle::extract<VUI, 1>(pair) : tcle::extract<VUI, 0>(pair);
}

/* Inclusive Hillis-Steele scan in log2(VEC_LEN) shift_right steps; returns the
   exclusive prefix (x shifted right by one lane) and the total in `total`. */
__device__ __forceinline__ VUI lane_exclusive_scan(VUI x, uint32_t &total) {
  VUI s = x;
  #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
  for (int d = 1; d < VEC_LEN; d <<= 1)
    s = s + tcle::shift_right(s, (unsigned int)d);
  total = static_cast<uint32_t>(s[VEC_LEN - 1]);
  return tcle::shift_right(s, 1u);
}

/* Compression table: dst[0..ret) = the indices of the chunks with a nonzero
   count, and ret (== comp_cnt) is returned. Stage 3 then walks comp_cnt slots
   instead of fc_al. counts is only read; dst is its own workspace leg. */
__device__ __forceinline__ int build_valid_table(uint32_t *counts,
                                                 int matrix_col, VUI viota_base,
                                                 uint32_t *dst) {
  int cnt = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < matrix_col; i++) {
    VUI counts_vec = tcle::load<VUI>(counts + i * VEC_LEN);
    VMASK nonempty = counts_vec != (VUI)(0);
    VUI st_offset = tcle::miota(nonempty) * (VUI)(sizeof(uint32_t));
    VUI vec_index = (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(i * VEC_LEN) + viota_base;
    tcle::scatter_cond(nonempty, vec_index, dst + cnt, st_offset);
    cnt += static_cast<int>(tcle::popc_mb<0>(nonempty));
  }
  return cnt;
}

template <int kMaxBaseNum, int kMaxShiftNum>
__device__ __forceinline__ bool find_k_bin(const uint32_t *hist, int &k_to_find,
                                           uint32_t &desired, int digit_pos) {
  int k = k_to_find;
  uint32_t d = desired;
  bool found = false;
  int last = kMaxBaseNum - 1;
  for (int i = last; i >= 0; i--) {
    int count = static_cast<int>(hist[i]);
    if (count == k) {
      d = ((d << DSA_BITS) | static_cast<uint32_t>(i))
          << ((kMaxShiftNum - 1 - digit_pos) * DSA_BITS);
      k = 0;
      found = true;
      break;
    }
    if (count > k) {
      d = (d << DSA_BITS) | static_cast<uint32_t>(i);
      break;
    }
    k -= count;
  }
  k_to_find = k;
  desired = d;
  return found;
}

template <int SHIFT, bool INIT>
static __device__ __noinline__ __attribute__((no_mem_alias_in_vldst_tar,
                                              loop_iterator_less_than_1024,
                                              enable_software_pipeliner,
                                              enable_bc_resolver))
void histogram_pass(uint32_t *su_ptr, int full_chunks, uint32_t desired) {
  VUI *vp = reinterpret_cast<VUI *>(su_ptr);
  tcle::leaptr<VUI> lp = tcle::simple_leaptr<VUI>(su_ptr);
  VUI vprefix = (VUI)(0);
  VUI vmask   = (VUI)(0);
  VUI vpad    = (VUI)(PAD_VALUE_DESC);
  if constexpr (!INIT) {
    vprefix = (VUI)(desired << ((SHIFT + 1) * DSA_BITS));
    vmask   = (VUI)(~0u << (DSA_BITS * (SHIFT + 1)));
  } else {
    (void)desired;
  }

  constexpr int UNROLL_CNT = 16;
  int c = 0;
  int c_batched = full_chunks & ~(UNROLL_CNT - 1);  // must be a power of 2
  VUI raw[UNROLL_CNT];
  for (; c < c_batched; c += UNROLL_CNT) {
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
      raw[j] = lp.load();
    if constexpr (!INIT) {
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j) {
        raw[j] = tcle::vsel((raw[j] & vmask) == vprefix, raw[j], vpad);
      }
    }
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j) {
      tcle::mradix_cntbase(raw[j], SHIFT);
    }
  }
  for (; c < full_chunks; c++) {
    VUI su = vp[c];
    if constexpr (!INIT)
      su = tcle::vsel(((su & vmask) == vprefix), su, vpad);
    tcle::mradix_cntbase(su, SHIFT);
  }
}

template <int... S>
static __device__ __forceinline__ void
dispatch_histogram_impl(int shift, uint32_t *su_ptr, int full_chunks,
                        uint32_t desired, std::integer_sequence<int, S...>) {
  ((shift == S ? (void)histogram_pass<S, false>(su_ptr, full_chunks, desired)
               : (void)0),
   ...);
}
static __device__ __forceinline__ void dispatch_histogram(int shift,
                                                          uint32_t *su_ptr,
                                                          int full_chunks,
                                                          uint32_t desired) {
  if (shift == MAX_SHIFT_NUM - 1) {
    histogram_pass<MAX_SHIFT_NUM - 1, true>(su_ptr, full_chunks, 0);
  } else {
    dispatch_histogram_impl(
        shift, su_ptr, full_chunks, desired,
        std::make_integer_sequence<int, MAX_SHIFT_NUM - 1>{});
  }
}

/* ============= Stage 2, 16-bit key domain =============
   The same digit walk as histogram_pass, on 256-lane u16 vectors: the band
   filter (&/==/vsel) covers 256 elements per op instead of 128, and a scan
   reads 2B per element instead of 4. MRADIX itself counts 32-bit lanes, so a
   vector is widened with one cvt into __vector2 unsigned int and counted as
   two VUI halves -- the count cost per element is unchanged, the traffic and
   the mask ops are not.
   INIT is the key's top digit (bit 15) only: nothing above it exists, so the
   band filter is skipped there. `desired` stays a key value, so the mask and
   prefix only ever use bits 15..5 and fit the narrow domain exactly. */
template <int SHIFT, bool INIT>
static __device__ __noinline__ __attribute__((no_mem_alias_in_vldst_tar,
                                              loop_iterator_less_than_1024,
                                              enable_software_pipeliner,
                                              enable_bc_resolver))
void histogram_pass16(const uint16_t *su16, int pairs16, uint32_t desired) {
  VUS *vp = reinterpret_cast<VUS *>(const_cast<uint16_t *>(su16));
  VUS vprefix = (VUS)(0);
  VUS vmask   = (VUS)(0);
  VUS vpad    = (VUS)(PAD_VALUE_DESC);
  if constexpr (!INIT) {
    vprefix = (VUS)(static_cast<uint16_t>(desired << ((SHIFT + 1) * DSA_BITS)));
    vmask   = (VUS)(static_cast<uint16_t>(0xFFFFu << (DSA_BITS * (SHIFT + 1))));
  } else {
    (void)desired;
  }

  constexpr int UNROLL_CNT = 16;
  int i = 0;
  int i_batched = pairs16 & ~(UNROLL_CNT - 1);  // must be a power of 2
  VUS raw[UNROLL_CNT];
  for (; i < i_batched; i += UNROLL_CNT) {
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
      raw[j] = vp[i + j];
    if constexpr (!INIT) {
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        raw[j] = tcle::vsel(((raw[j] & vmask) == vprefix), raw[j], vpad);
    }
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < UNROLL_CNT; ++j) {
      VUI2 w = tcle::cvt<VUI2>(raw[j]);
      tcle::mradix_cntbase(tcle::extract<VUI, 0>(w), SHIFT);
      tcle::mradix_cntbase(tcle::extract<VUI, 1>(w), SHIFT);
    }
  }
  for (; i < pairs16; i++) {
    VUS v = vp[i];
    if constexpr (!INIT)
      v = tcle::vsel(((v & vmask) == vprefix), v, vpad);
    VUI2 w = tcle::cvt<VUI2>(v);
    tcle::mradix_cntbase(tcle::extract<VUI, 0>(w), SHIFT);
    tcle::mradix_cntbase(tcle::extract<VUI, 1>(w), SHIFT);
  }
}

template <int... S>
static __device__ __forceinline__ void
dispatch_histogram16_impl(int shift, const uint16_t *su16, int pairs16,
                          uint32_t desired, std::integer_sequence<int, S...>) {
  ((shift == S ? (void)histogram_pass16<S, false>(su16, pairs16, desired)
               : (void)0),
   ...);
}
static __device__ __forceinline__ void dispatch_histogram16(
    int shift, const uint16_t *su16, int pairs16, uint32_t desired) {
  if (shift == FIRST_DIGIT16) {
    histogram_pass16<FIRST_DIGIT16, true>(su16, pairs16, 0);
  } else {
    dispatch_histogram16_impl(shift, su16, pairs16, desired,
                              std::make_integer_sequence<int, FIRST_DIGIT16>{});
  }
}

/* ============= Stage-3 lane scatter =============
 * One masked 16-lane table load plus gathers replace per-chunk scalar metadata
 * reads; every kept lane then lands in its own staging slot via scatter_cond.
 *
 * The split path must survive duplicate k-th values with exactly K staging
 * slots: the ge stream alone can neither be truncated by position (a late gt
 * lane must survive even when earlier eq lanes fill the buffer) nor overflow
 * the buffer. So the two predicates write disjoint ranges, in one pass, from
 * the prefixes stage 2 already built:
 *     gt lane -> [0, n_gt)                              unconditional
 *     eq lane -> [n_gt + eq_prefix[c], ... + rank)      while < K
 * where eq_prefix[c] = ge_prefix[c] - gt_prefix[c] counts the eq lanes of the
 * chunks before c, so staging[0, K) is the final answer and no post-scatter
 * trim is needed.
 * The scatter target is staging, not the global row, so that the K values and
 * the K indices stay in one place: the launcher's S5 read takes both from here,
 * and a later S4 sort could rewrite the pair in place.
 *
 * `off` is the element offset where the selected lanes get packed; the
 * 16-batched path gathers it (v_ge_off[j] / v_gt_off[j]) while the
 * 4/remainder path reads it scalar.
 */

/* Chunk accessor for the scatter sites: chunk c's VEC_LEN keys, in the same
   domain `vkey` lives in, plus the fp32 bit patterns those keys encode (what
   staging, S4 and the launcher's S5 expect). kind == 0 is the fp32 path, where
   the row already holds fp32 keys; 1/2 are the fp16/bf16 paths, where the row
   holds 16-bit keys and one 256-lane vector holds two chunks. */
static __device__ __forceinline__ void chunk_vals(const uint32_t *su32,
                                                  const uint16_t *su16, int kind,
                                                  int c, VUI &keys, VUI &fbits) {
  if (kind == 0) {
    keys = reinterpret_cast<const VUI *>(su32)[c];
    fbits = UT2VT(keys);
  } else {
    VUS pair = load_pair16(su16, c);
    VUI2 w = tcle::cvt<VUI2>(pair);
    keys = chunk_keys16(w, c);
    if (kind == 2) {
      /* bf16: key16 << 16 already is the fp32 key, so one fold gives the value
         bits with no extra traffic. */
      fbits = key16_to_bf16_bits(keys);
    } else {
      /* fp16: restore the pattern, then one hardware cvt covers both halves.
         The cvt is exact for subnormals, which an integer rebuild is not. */
      VF2 f = tcle::cvt<VF2>(tcle::bit_cast<VF16>(UT2VT16(pair)));
      VF half = (c & 1) ? tcle::extract<VF, 1>(f) : tcle::extract<VF, 0>(f);
      fbits = tcle::bit_cast<VUI>(half);
    }
  }
}

/* Fast path: n_ge == K, so the ge stream IS the answer. One mask, two scatters
   per lane. */
__device__ __forceinline__ void scatter_lane(VUI su, VUI fbits, VUI vkey, int c,
                                             int off, VUI viota_base,
                                             float *stage_v, int *stage_i) {
  VUI idx_vec = viota_base + (VUI)(c * VEC_LEN);
  VMASK mask = CmpGe()(su, vkey);
  VUI bytes = tcle::miota(mask) * (VUI)(sizeof(float));
  tcle::scatter_cond(mask, fbits, stage_v + off, bytes);
  tcle::scatter_cond(mask, idx_vec, stage_i + off, bytes);
}

/* Slow path: gt lanes first, then the eq lanes that still fit.
   eq_off = n_gt + ge_off - gt_off (eq = ge - gt). The vsel turns the eq mask
   into a rank vector with 0xFFFFFFFF for non-eq lanes, so one compare
   expresses "eq lane AND rank < K - eq_off". Empty masks no-op, so an eq chunk
   past the K budget costs one compare. */
__device__ __forceinline__ void scatter_split_lane(
    VUI su, VUI fbits, VUI vkey, int c, int gt_off, int ge_off, int n_gt, int K,
    VUI viota_base, float *stage_v, int *stage_i) {
  VUI idx_vec = viota_base + (VUI)(c * VEC_LEN);
  VMASK gt_mask = CmpGt()(su, vkey);
  VUI gt_bytes = tcle::miota(gt_mask) * (VUI)(sizeof(float));
  tcle::scatter_cond(gt_mask, fbits, stage_v + gt_off, gt_bytes);
  tcle::scatter_cond(gt_mask, idx_vec, stage_i + gt_off, gt_bytes);
  int eq_off = n_gt + ge_off - gt_off;
  int eq_room = K - eq_off;
  int eq_dst = eq_room > 0 ? eq_off : 0;
  uint32_t eq_room_u = eq_room > 0 ? static_cast<uint32_t>(eq_room) : 0u;
  VMASK eq_mask = CmpEq()(su, vkey);
  VUI eq_iota = tcle::miota(eq_mask);
  VUI keep_v = tcle::vsel(eq_mask, eq_iota, (VUI)(0xFFFFFFFFu));
  VMASK keep = (keep_v < (VUI)(eq_room_u));
  VUI eq_bytes = eq_iota * (VUI)(sizeof(float));
  tcle::scatter_cond(keep, fbits, stage_v + eq_dst, eq_bytes);
  tcle::scatter_cond(keep, idx_vec, stage_i + eq_dst, eq_bytes);
}

/* ---- slow path: ties at the K-th value -----------------------------------
   Must stay __forceinline__: an out-of-line callee takes l1_buf as an opaque
   argument and loses the base specialization the scatter sites rely on.

   Contract: every subthread calls it (the branch is uniform after the stage-2
   barrier) with its own chunk window [chunk_start, chunk_start + chunk_count)
   and it performs its own barriers. It writes the gt prefix into gt_prefix,
   publishes n_gt into totals[0], then scatters
       gt lane -> [0, n_gt)                              unconditional
       eq lane -> [n_gt + eq_prefix[c], ... + rank)      only while < K
   so stage_v/stage_i[0, K) ends up holding exactly the top-K. */
static __device__ __forceinline__ void reclaim_scatter_split(
    uint32_t *su_ptr, const uint16_t *su16, int kind, uint32_t key_uint, int K,
    int matrix_col, int comp_cnt, int sub_idx, int sub_num, int chunk_start,
    int chunk_count, uint32_t *ge_prefix, uint32_t *gt_counts,
    uint32_t *gt_prefix, uint32_t *valid_vec_idx, float *stage_v, int *stage_i,
    uint32_t *totals, VUI viota_base) {
  VUI vkey = (VUI)(key_uint);

  // S3 stage 1b: gt (> key) counts. The pad lanes [full_chunks, fc_al) are
  // not re-zeroed here: the caller's stage 1 already zeroed both count legs.
  for (int c = chunk_start; c < chunk_start + chunk_count; c++) {
    VUI keys, fbits;
    chunk_vals(su_ptr, su16, kind, c, keys, fbits);
    gt_counts[c] = tcle::popc_mb<0>(keys > vkey);
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  // S3 stage 2b: gt exclusive prefix + total (sub0).
  if (sub_idx == 0) {
    VUI running_gt = (VUI)(0);
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix_col; i++) {
      VUI tv = tcle::loadeoa<VUI>(gt_counts + i, matrix_col);
      tcle::storeeoa(running_gt, gt_prefix + i, matrix_col);
      running_gt = running_gt + tv;
    }
    uint32_t acc_t = 0;
    VUI base_gt = lane_exclusive_scan(running_gt, acc_t);
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix_col; i++) {
      VUI tv = tcle::loadeoa<VUI>(gt_prefix + i, matrix_col);
      tcle::storeeoa(tv + base_gt, gt_prefix + i, matrix_col);
    }
    totals[0] = acc_t;
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  int n_gt = static_cast<int>(totals[0]);

  /* S3 stage 3b: two-stream scatter, same 16-batched gather form as the fast
     path (repeat16 / repeat4 / remainder over the compressed table). */
  int repeat16 = comp_cnt / (sub_num * 16);
  int remainder16 = comp_cnt % (sub_num * 16);
  int repeat4 = remainder16 / (sub_num * 4);
  int remainder4 = remainder16 % (sub_num * 4);
  uint32_t bpe = static_cast<uint32_t>(sizeof(uint32_t));
  for (int idx = 0; idx < repeat16; idx++) {
    int offset = idx * sub_num * 16 + sub_idx * 16;
    VUI vec_c = tcle::load<VUI>(valid_vec_idx + offset, (VUI)(0),
                                tcle::vset_mb<VMASK>(16));
    VUI v_ge_off = tcle::gather<VUI>(ge_prefix, vec_c * bpe);
    VUI v_gt_off = tcle::gather<VUI>(gt_prefix, vec_c * bpe);
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {
      int c = static_cast<int>(vec_c[j]);
      VUI keys, fbits;
      chunk_vals(su_ptr, su16, kind, c, keys, fbits);
      scatter_split_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(v_gt_off[j]),
                         static_cast<int>(v_ge_off[j]), n_gt, K, viota_base,
                         stage_v, stage_i);
    }
  }
  for (int idx = 0; idx < repeat4; idx++) {
    int offset = repeat16 * sub_num * 16 + idx * sub_num * 4 + sub_idx * 4;
    for (int u = 0; u < 4; u++) {
      int c = static_cast<int>(valid_vec_idx[offset + u]);
      VUI keys, fbits;
      chunk_vals(su_ptr, su16, kind, c, keys, fbits);
      scatter_split_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(gt_prefix[c]),
                         static_cast<int>(ge_prefix[c]), n_gt, K, viota_base,
                         stage_v, stage_i);
    }
  }
  for (int idx = sub_idx; idx < remainder4; idx += sub_num) {
    int offset = repeat16 * sub_num * 16 + repeat4 * sub_num * 4 + idx;
    int c = static_cast<int>(valid_vec_idx[offset]);
    VUI keys, fbits;
    chunk_vals(su_ptr, su16, kind, c, keys, fbits);
    scatter_split_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(gt_prefix[c]),
                       static_cast<int>(ge_prefix[c]), n_gt, K, viota_base,
                       stage_v, stage_i);
  }
}

/* ============= Stage-4 sorted output =============
 * S3 leaves the row's k elements in staging in reclaimed order, which is the
 * torch.topk(sorted=False) contract. S4 turns that into the sorted=True
 * contract with a bitonic argsort of the staged (key, index) pairs:
 *
 *   - pairs are ordered by the sort-uint key (VT2UT of the float bits, the
 *     same monotone map S1 uses) descending, ties by ascending row position,
 *     which is the order torch.topk returns for equal values;
 *   - the sort width is next_pow2(K), not a whole vector: the network cost is
 *     one stage per (bitonic level, distance, chunk), so a K=8 row pays 6
 *     stages rather than the 28 a 128-lane network would need. Lanes [K, width)
 *     are padded with the smallest possible key and an index above every real
 *     position, so they can never displace a real lane;
 *   - a distance below VEC_LEN pairs two lanes of one vector and is reached
 *     with two lane shifts plus a select (GCU has no lane shuffle). The
 *     cheaper-looking alternative -- publish the tile and tcle::gather
 *     `lane ^ d` -- is NOT usable: a vector store followed by a gather of the
 *     same staging address comes back stale (measured: every shape wrong),
 *     and the barrier that would order them is a whole-subthread barrier that
 *     cannot sit inside this sub0-only stage. A pair of lane shifts instead
 *     costs ~0.14us per stage per chunk, which is what the width above keeps
 *     small. A distance of VEC_LEN or more pairs whole chunks, which stay in
 *     registers.
 *
 * Width and chunk count are template parameters, so every index in the network
 * folds to a constant and the tiles stay in vector registers: a per-stage
 * round trip through staging is not ordered against itself (this function
 * carries no_mem_alias_in_vldst_tar), and the measured symptom of that is a
 * network that silently sorts stale lanes.
 *
 * Only subthread 0 runs it: a cross-chunk distance needs both chunks of the
 * pair anyway, so spreading the chunks over subthreads would cost one barrier
 * per stage and gain at most 2x at the largest K.
 */
static __device__ __forceinline__ VMASK pair_gt(VUI va, VUI ia, VUI vb,
                                               VUI ib) {
  return (va > vb) | ((va == vb) & (ia < ib));
}

/* Compare-exchange one chunk pair: the low chunk keeps the maximum unless the
   block ascends. A distance of at least VEC_LEN implies k > VEC_LEN, so
   `lane & k` is constant over a whole chunk and the direction is a scalar. */
static __device__ __forceinline__ void pair_chunk(VUI &v0, VUI &i0, VUI &v1,
                                                  VUI &i1, bool asc) {
  VMASK g = pair_gt(v0, i0, v1, i1);
  VUI vmax = tcle::vsel(g, v0, v1);
  VUI imax = tcle::vsel(g, i0, i1);
  VUI vmin = tcle::vsel(g, v1, v0);
  VUI imin = tcle::vsel(g, i1, i0);
  v0 = asc ? vmin : vmax;
  i0 = asc ? imin : imax;
  v1 = asc ? vmax : vmin;
  i1 = asc ? imax : imin;
}

template <int WIDTH>
static __device__ __forceinline__ void sort_staged_pairs(uint32_t *stage_v,
                                                         uint32_t *stage_i,
                                                         int K, VUI lane) {
  // Whole vectors covered by the network: 1 for WIDTH <= VEC_LEN.
  constexpr int NCHUNK = (WIDTH + VEC_LEN - 1) / VEC_LEN;
  const VUI vzero = (VUI)0;
  const VUI vone = (VUI)1;
  /* Fixed size so that a branch that is dead for this WIDTH still indexes
     inside the array; only [0, NCHUNK) is ever touched. */
  VUI v[4];
  VUI i[4];

  /* (a) float bits -> sortable keys, and pad lanes [K, width). */
  #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
  for (int c = 0; c < NCHUNK; ++c) {
    VUI lane_c = lane + (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(c * VEC_LEN);
    VMASK live = lane_c < (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(K);
    v[c] = tcle::vsel(live, VT2UT(tcle::load<VUI>(stage_v + c * VEC_LEN)),
                      vzero);
    i[c] = tcle::vsel(live, tcle::load<VUI>(stage_i + c * VEC_LEN),
                      (VUI)(0x7fffffffu) - lane_c);
  }

  /* (b) bitonic network; `asc` is the mirror image of the textbook rule so the
     whole array ends up descending. */
  #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
  for (int k = 2; k <= WIDTH; k <<= 1) {
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int d = k >> 1; d > 0; d >>= 1) {
      if (d < VEC_LEN) {
        /* shift_right gives v[i - d] and shift_left v[i + d]; each lane selects
           the side its partner is on. Both are in range for the selected side
           (i with bit d clear has i + d < VEC_LEN, i with it set has i - d >= 0),
           so the shift's out-of-range fill never reaches the network. */
        VMASK has_d = (lane & (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(d)) != vzero;
        #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
        for (int c = 0; c < NCHUNK; ++c) {
          VMASK asc = ((lane + (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(c * VEC_LEN)) &
                       (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(k)) != vzero;
          VUI vv = v[c];
          VUI ii = i[c];
          VUI ov = tcle::vsel(has_d,
                              tcle::shift_right(vv, static_cast<unsigned>(d)),
                              tcle::shift_left(vv, static_cast<unsigned>(d)));
          VUI oi = tcle::vsel(has_d,
                              tcle::shift_right(ii, static_cast<unsigned>(d)),
                              tcle::shift_left(ii, static_cast<unsigned>(d)));
          /* The low lane of a pair keeps the maximum when the block is
             descending; take the partner when this lane does not hold it. */
          VUI keep_max =
              tcle::vsel(has_d, vzero, vone) ^ tcle::vsel(asc, vone, vzero);
          VMASK take = (tcle::vsel(pair_gt(vv, ii, ov, oi), vone, vzero) ^
                        keep_max) != vzero;
          v[c] = tcle::vsel(take, ov, vv);
          i[c] = tcle::vsel(take, oi, ii);
        }
      } else if (d == VEC_LEN) {
        if (NCHUNK >= 2)
          pair_chunk(v[0], i[0], v[1], i[1], (0 * VEC_LEN & k) != 0);
        if (NCHUNK >= 4)
          pair_chunk(v[2], i[2], v[3], i[3], (2 * VEC_LEN & k) != 0);
      } else {
        if (NCHUNK >= 4) {
          pair_chunk(v[0], i[0], v[2], i[2], (0 * VEC_LEN & k) != 0);
          pair_chunk(v[1], i[1], v[3], i[3], (1 * VEC_LEN & k) != 0);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  /* (c) keys back to float bits (the launcher's read is unchanged) and both
     streams back to staging, now in sorted=True order. */
  #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
  for (int c = 0; c < NCHUNK; ++c) {
    tcle::store(UT2VT(v[c]), stage_v + c * VEC_LEN);
    tcle::store(i[c], stage_i + c * VEC_LEN);
  }
}

/* Dispatch on the width the caller's K needs; S4 only runs for K <= 4 * VEC_LEN
   (SORT_K_MAX in the launcher). */
static __device__ __forceinline__ void sort_staged(uint32_t *stage_v,
                                                   uint32_t *stage_i, int K,
                                                   VUI lane) {
  int width = 2;
  while (width < K)
    width <<= 1;
  if (width <= VEC_LEN) {
    switch (width) {
    case 2:
      sort_staged_pairs<2>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    case 4:
      sort_staged_pairs<4>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    case 8:
      sort_staged_pairs<8>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    case 16:
      sort_staged_pairs<16>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    case 32:
      sort_staged_pairs<32>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    case 64:
      sort_staged_pairs<64>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    default:
      sort_staged_pairs<VEC_LEN>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
      break;
    }
  } else if (width <= 2 * VEC_LEN) {
    sort_staged_pairs<2 * VEC_LEN>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
  } else {
    sort_staged_pairs<4 * VEC_LEN>(stage_v, stage_i, K, lane);
  }
}

/* ---- S4 radix variant ---------------------------------------------------
   The bitonic network above sorts in place but costs O(log^2 K) dependent
   vector stages on one subthread; for K = 128/256 that was 33-35% of the whole
   row (torch/topsop pays 13-21%). topsop's sorted=True instead runs an LSD
   radix sort on the hardware MRADIX unit (radix_sort_bpe4), so this variant
   does the same: one 5-bit digit per pass, count with mradix_cntbase, turn the
   per-bin counts into a descending base, then mradix_offset + tcle::scatter
   place every lane. Seven passes cover the 32-bit staging key (5-bit digits,
   LSB first); the 16-bit input paths reconstruct an exact fp32 value in S3, so
   their keys span the full 32-bit grid here too.
   The sort is stable, so equal keys keep the order S3 paged them in, which is
   exactly the bitonic network's explicit ascending-index tie-break: the fast
   (n_ge == K) path pages ascending row position, and the split path stages
   [gt][eq] where equal keys never straddle the two groups (gt > key == eq).
   The pass needs a destination disjoint from its source; the caller passes a
   scratch window (see the S4 banner) and only reaches here when one fits. */
template <int SHIFT>
static __device__ __forceinline__ void radix_pass(uint32_t *dst_v,
                                                  uint32_t *dst_i,
                                                  uint32_t *src_v,
                                                  uint32_t *src_i,
                                                  int nvec) {
  constexpr int BATCH = 8;
  const VUI vzero = (VUI)0;

  tcle::mradix_w_base(vzero);
  int c = 0;
  for (; c + BATCH <= nvec; c += BATCH) {
    VUI v[BATCH];
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < BATCH; ++j)
      v[j] = tcle::load<VUI>(src_v + (c + j) * VEC_LEN);
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < BATCH; ++j)
      tcle::mradix_cntbase(v[j], SHIFT);
  }
  for (; c < nvec; ++c)
    tcle::mradix_cntbase(tcle::load<VUI>(src_v + c * VEC_LEN), SHIFT);

  /* Descending base: bin 31 (the largest digit) starts at 0, then bin 30, ...
     so the scatter writes the largest keys first. One scalar array is enough:
     the exclusive suffix sum overwrites the counts in place. */
  __valigned__ uint32_t bins[VEC_LEN];
  tcle::store(tcle::mradix_r_base<VUI>(), bins);
  uint32_t acc = 0;
  #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
  for (int i = 31; i >= 0; --i) {
    uint32_t cnt = bins[i];
    bins[i] = acc;
    acc += cnt;
  }
  tcle::mradix_w_base(
      tcle::load<VUI>(bins, vzero, tcle::vset_mb<VMASK>(32)));

  c = 0;
  for (; c + BATCH <= nvec; c += BATCH) {
    VUI v[BATCH], ix[BATCH], off[BATCH];
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < BATCH; ++j) {
      v[j] = tcle::load<VUI>(src_v + (c + j) * VEC_LEN);
      ix[j] = tcle::load<VUI>(src_i + (c + j) * VEC_LEN);
    }
    /* mradix_offset is stateful and advances the per-bin running position, so
       the offsets must be taken in vector order before any scatter. */
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < BATCH; ++j)
      off[j] = tcle::mradix_offset(v[j], SHIFT);
    #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
    for (int j = 0; j < BATCH; ++j) {
      tcle::scatter(v[j], dst_v, off[j]);
      tcle::scatter(ix[j], dst_i, off[j]);
    }
  }
  for (; c < nvec; ++c) {
    VUI v = tcle::load<VUI>(src_v + c * VEC_LEN);
    VUI ix = tcle::load<VUI>(src_i + c * VEC_LEN);
    VUI off = tcle::mradix_offset(v, SHIFT);
    tcle::scatter(v, dst_v, off);
    tcle::scatter(ix, dst_i, off);
  }
}

static __device__ __forceinline__ void sort_staged_radix(uint32_t *stage_v,
                                                         uint32_t *stage_i,
                                                         uint32_t *scr_v,
                                                         uint32_t *scr_i,
                                                         int K, int nvec,
                                                         VUI lane) {
  const VUI vzero = (VUI)0;
  const VUI vpad_i = (VUI)(0xffffffffu);

  /* Fill the scratch with monotonic keys and pad [K, nvec*VEC_LEN) to the
     bottom of the key order (0 sorts last descending, and the pad index is the
     largest possible so a real key 0 still outranks a pad lane). */
  for (int c = 0; c < nvec; ++c) {
    VUI lane_c = lane + (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(c * VEC_LEN);
    VMASK live = lane_c < (VUI)static_cast<uint32_t>(K);
    VUI kv = VT2UT(tcle::load<VUI>(stage_v + c * VEC_LEN));
    VUI iv = tcle::load<VUI>(stage_i + c * VEC_LEN);
    tcle::store(tcle::vsel(live, kv, vzero), scr_v + c * VEC_LEN);
    tcle::store(tcle::vsel(live, iv, vpad_i), scr_i + c * VEC_LEN);
  }

  /* Seven passes, LSB digit first; the odd count leaves the result in the
     staging buffers, so only the key->float-bits restore remains. */
  radix_pass<0>(stage_v, stage_i, scr_v, scr_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<1>(scr_v, scr_i, stage_v, stage_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<2>(stage_v, stage_i, scr_v, scr_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<3>(scr_v, scr_i, stage_v, stage_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<4>(stage_v, stage_i, scr_v, scr_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<5>(scr_v, scr_i, stage_v, stage_i, nvec);
  radix_pass<6>(stage_v, stage_i, scr_v, scr_i, nvec);

  for (int c = 0; c < nvec; ++c)
    tcle::store(UT2VT(tcle::load<VUI>(stage_v + c * VEC_LEN)),
                stage_v + c * VEC_LEN);
}

// ============= kernel entry: one row per call (persistent on the Struct side)

extern "C" __device__ __attribute__((no_mem_alias_in_vldst_tar,
                                     loop_iterator_less_than_1024,
                                     enable_software_pipeliner,
                                     enable_bc_resolver))
void topk_mradix_l1_kernel(
    uint32_t *l1_buf,       // row buffer, BLOCK_N words (>= next_pow2(N))
    uint32_t *reclaim_ws_in,// reclaim workspace, WS_SIZE words
    int32_t N, int32_t K,
    int32_t is_sorted,      // 1 = leave the k elements in sorted=True order
    int32_t in_kind) {      // 0 = staged fp32, 1 = fp16, 2 = bf16 (staged raw)

  /* What the launcher staged (see topk_mradix.py): a fp32 row, or a raw
     fp16/bf16 row (2B per element, half the HBM read and half the S1/S2 scan
     traffic). l1_buf is one window of BLOCK_N elements of that dtype; this
     entry reinterprets it, so the pointer's element type is irrelevant to it.
     The three paths differ only in the key width and in how a key turns
     back into a value, so everything below branches on in_kind once per
     stage instead of duplicating the pipeline. */
  const bool k16 = (in_kind != 0);
  int32_t vec_len = k16 ? VEC_LEN16 : VEC_LEN;

  int32_t N_padded = (N + vec_len - 1) / vec_len * vec_len;
  int32_t full_chunks = N_padded / VEC_LEN;
  /* 16-bit rows are staged behind a 256-lane vector, so a pad lane up to
     BLOCK_N may be scanned: the launcher pads the whole window with -inf. */
  int32_t pairs16 = N_padded / VEC_LEN16;

  // Subthread index/count; the kernel assumes sub_num == 4 (see the header).
  int32_t sub_idx = subThreadIdx.x;
  int32_t sub_num = threadDim.x;

  /* MRADIX/DTE and vector ops require the per-SIP vector length to be set.
     Without it the MRADIX engine and DTE-triggered VDMEM transfers fault at
     runtime.
     The VL must also match the width of the vectors the row is scanned with:
     a fp16/bf16 row travels as 256-lane u16 vectors (VEC_LEN16), a fp32 row as
     128-lane u32 vectors (VEC_LEN). popc_mb/miota/cvt read a mask back at
     min(mask width, VL), so a VL below the lane count silently truncates every
     256-lane op to its low half. A narrower vector is unaffected by a larger
     VL, so the 16-bit path runs at VEC_LEN16 end to end. */
  tcle::set_vl(k16 ? VEC_LEN16 : VEC_LEN);

  // Hardware vector iota instead of a VEC_LEN-iteration scalar init loop.
  VUI viota_base = tcle::mid<VUI, 0>(0);

  /* OWNERSHIP: both VDMEM windows are allocated by the TLE-Struct launcher
     (tle.gpu.alloc + tle.gpu.local_ptr) and arrive here as plain pointers. A
     raw region must not declare a __local__ array of its own in this form:
     raw static buffers and tle.gpu.alloc both start at VDMEM offset 0, and
     neither side knows about the other, so a __local__ buffer would silently
     overlap the launcher's window.
       l1_buf      holds one row: BLOCK_N elements of the input dtype -- fp32,
                   or raw fp16/bf16 for in_kind != 0 -- then its sort keys in
                   place (uint32 / uint16 resp.; BLOCK_N = max(128, 256,
                   next_pow2(N)) elements, i.e. <= 256KB for fp32 and <= 128KB
                   for a 16-bit row). The launcher fills it with tle.gpu.copy
                   before the call.
       reclaim_ws  counts / prefixes / compression table / staging; its layout
                   and capacity are pinned by the WS_* constants above.
     One row per call, so there is no parity to alternate and no prefetch
     state: the row loop, the copy and the writeback all live in the launcher.
     This entry issues no DTE command at all. */

  uint32_t *l1_buf_ = l1_buf;
  uint32_t *reclaim_ws = reclaim_ws_in;

  // ======================================================================
  // S1  VT2UT (in place)
  //     The row is already in l1_buf: the TLE-Struct launcher staged it with
  //     tle.gpu.copy (mask = offs < N, pad = -inf). Every subthread now
  //     converts an equal share of it in place -- 256-lane u16 vectors for a
  //     16-bit row, VEC_LEN-lane u32 vectors for a fp32 one; this is where the
  //     boundary sits, so nothing below depends on how the row arrived.
  // ======================================================================
  uint32_t *su_ptr = l1_buf_;
  uint16_t *su16 = reinterpret_cast<uint16_t *>(l1_buf_);
  const uint16_t *cu16 = reinterpret_cast<const uint16_t *>(l1_buf_);

  // full_chunks covers N_padded, so the last chunk's tail lanes are converted
  // here too; the pad lanes [N, N_padded) map to 0x007fffff (see the pad note
  // above), below every finite key. The 16-bit path does the same thing per
  // 256-lane vector, in place and with the same proof: a fp16/bf16 pattern's
  // image under VT2UT16 still sorts below every finite key.
  if (k16) {
    VUS *vp16 = reinterpret_cast<VUS *>(su16);
    int gps = pairs16 / sub_num;
    int grem = pairs16 % sub_num;
    int g0 = sub_idx * gps + std::min(sub_idx, grem);
    int gn = gps + (sub_idx < grem ? 1 : 0);
    int g = g0;
    int g_end = g0 + gn;
    constexpr int VT2UT16_UNROLL_CNT = 8;
    int g_batched = g + (gn & ~(VT2UT16_UNROLL_CNT - 1));  // power of 2
    for (; g < g_batched; g += VT2UT16_UNROLL_CNT) {
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < VT2UT16_UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        vp16[g + j] = VT2UT16(vp16[g + j]);
    }
    for (; g < g_end; g++) {
      vp16[g] = VT2UT16(vp16[g]);
    }
  } else {
    int chunks_per_sub = full_chunks / sub_num;
    int chunks_rem     = full_chunks % sub_num;
    int chunk_start = sub_idx * chunks_per_sub
                      + (sub_idx < chunks_rem ? sub_idx : chunks_rem);
    int chunk_count = chunks_per_sub + (sub_idx < chunks_rem ? 1 : 0);

    VUI *vp = reinterpret_cast<VUI *>(su_ptr);
    int c = chunk_start;
    int c_end = chunk_start + chunk_count;
    // Unrolled so several VEC_LEN-lane loads are in flight; best of
    // 1/2/4/8/16/32 on three shapes (16: +0.8..2.4%, 32: +5%).
    constexpr int VT2UT_UNROLL_CNT = 8;
    int c_batched = c + (chunk_count & ~(VT2UT_UNROLL_CNT - 1));  // power of 2
    for (; c < c_batched; c += VT2UT_UNROLL_CNT) {
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < VT2UT_UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        vp[c + j] = VT2UT(vp[c + j]);
    }
    for (; c < c_end; c++) {
      vp[c] = VT2UT(vp[c]);
    }
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  // ======================================================================
  // S2  histogram + find_k_bin  (sub0 only: MRADIX is a shared HW unit)
  //     One MRADIX digit pass per 5-bit digit, MSB first (7 digits for a fp32
  //     key, 4 for a fp16/bf16 one), each narrowing the candidate prefix;
  //     find_k_bin then walks the 32 bins to pick the digit holding the k-th
  //     largest element.
  //     Leaves `desired` = sort key of the k-th largest value,
  //     the same in every subthread after the barrier.
  // ======================================================================
  uint32_t desired = 0;
  if (sub_idx == 0) {
    bool found_k = false;
    int k_to_find = K;
    /* The bins are read back through this aligned stack array, not by
       indexing the OACC vector directly: a lane read lowers to v.movoa2r
       (OACC -> scalar-register move, one per bin) and measured 5-12%
       slower than one 512B vector store plus 32 constant-address scalar
       loads, which the compiler hoists and issues in parallel. */
    __valigned__ uint32_t hist_stack[VEC_LEN];  // one VUI; 32 bins

    for (int digit_pos = (k16 ? FIRST_DIGIT16 : 0);
         digit_pos < MAX_SHIFT_NUM && !found_k; digit_pos++) {
      int shift = MAX_SHIFT_NUM - 1 - digit_pos;
      VUI vzero = (VUI)(0);
      tcle::mradix_w_base(vzero);

      if (k16) {
        dispatch_histogram16(shift, cu16, pairs16, desired);
      } else {
        dispatch_histogram(shift, su_ptr, full_chunks, desired);
      }

      VUI hist_result = tcle::mradix_r_base<VUI>();
      tcle::store(hist_result, hist_stack);

      found_k = find_k_bin<NUM_BINS, MAX_SHIFT_NUM>(hist_stack, k_to_find,
                                                    desired, digit_pos);
    }
    /* Sublthread 0 publishes desired in ws[4095]; every subthread reads it
       right after the barrier below, and the slot is only rewritten (with
       n_ge) after the next barrier, so the reuse is safe. */
    reclaim_ws[4095] = desired;
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  if (sub_idx != 0)
    desired = reclaim_ws[4095];

  // ======================================================================
  // S3  reclaim: select the top-k lanes into staging
  //     Per-chunk popcount of the ge (>= threshold) stream (all subthreads),
  //     exclusive prefix over the chunks plus a compression table of the
  //     non-empty ones (sub0/sub2), then the lane scatter into staging
  //     (all). Fast path n_ge == K (unique floats): the ge stream is the
  //     answer. Slow path n_ge > K (ties at the k-th value): recount with
  //     '>', then scatter gt to [0, n_gt) and eq to [n_gt, K).
  // ======================================================================
  VUI vkey = (VUI)(desired);

  /* S3 stage 1 -- per-chunk ge (>= key) counts, all subthreads, straight into
     the L1 workspace. The split path recounts the gt stream inside
     reclaim_scatter_split, so the row is never swept a third time. A 16-bit
     row is read as 256-lane vectors and yields two adjacent chunk counts, so
     the counts keep the VEC_LEN granularity below.
     Legs of fc_al = ALIGN_UP(full_chunks, VEC_LEN) words each (see the WS_*
     layout at the top of the file): [ge_counts][ge_prefix][gt_counts]
     [gt_prefix][valid_vec_idx]. eq_prefix is not stored -- eq = ge - gt, stage
     3 derives it -- and fc_al rounds up to VEC_LEN because stage 2 moves whole
     VEC_LEN-lane columns, so a leg that is not a whole number of columns would
     have its last column store run into the next leg. */
  int fc_al = (full_chunks + VEC_LEN - 1) & ~(VEC_LEN - 1);
  uint32_t *ge_counts = reclaim_ws;
  uint32_t *ge_prefix = reclaim_ws + fc_al;
  uint32_t *gt_counts = reclaim_ws + 2 * fc_al;
  uint32_t *gt_prefix = reclaim_ws + 3 * fc_al;
  uint32_t *valid_vec_idx = reclaim_ws + 4 * fc_al;
  VUI *vp = reinterpret_cast<VUI *>(su_ptr);
  int chunk_start = sub_idx * (full_chunks / sub_num)
                    + std::min(sub_idx, full_chunks % sub_num);
  int chunk_count = full_chunks / sub_num + (sub_idx < full_chunks % sub_num);
  if (k16) {
    /* One 256-lane load yields two adjacent 128-chunk counts, so the counts
       keep the VEC_LEN granularity the prefix/scatter code expects while the
       row is read at 2B per element. full_chunks is even here: N_padded is a
       multiple of VEC_LEN16. */
    const VUS *g16 = reinterpret_cast<const VUS *>(cu16);
    int pair_start = sub_idx * (pairs16 / sub_num)
                     + std::min(sub_idx, pairs16 % sub_num);
    int pair_count = pairs16 / sub_num + (sub_idx < pairs16 % sub_num);
    constexpr int GE16_UNROLL_CNT = 8;
    int p = pair_start;
    int p_end = pair_start + pair_count;
    int p_batched = p + (pair_count & ~(GE16_UNROLL_CNT - 1));  // power of 2
    for (; p < p_batched; p += GE16_UNROLL_CNT) {
      VUS s[GE16_UNROLL_CNT];
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < GE16_UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        s[j] = g16[p + j];
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < GE16_UNROLL_CNT; ++j) {
        VUI2 w = tcle::cvt<VUI2>(s[j]);
        ge_counts[2 * (p + j)] =
            tcle::popc_mb<0>(tcle::extract<VUI, 0>(w) >= vkey);
        ge_counts[2 * (p + j) + 1] =
            tcle::popc_mb<0>(tcle::extract<VUI, 1>(w) >= vkey);
      }
    }
    for (; p < p_end; p++) {
      VUI2 w = tcle::cvt<VUI2>(g16[p]);
      ge_counts[2 * p] = tcle::popc_mb<0>(tcle::extract<VUI, 0>(w) >= vkey);
      ge_counts[2 * p + 1] = tcle::popc_mb<0>(tcle::extract<VUI, 1>(w) >= vkey);
    }
  } else {
    constexpr int GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT = 16;
    int c = chunk_start;
    int c_end = chunk_start + chunk_count;
    int c_batched = c + (chunk_count & ~(GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT - 1));  // power of 2
    for (; c < c_batched; c += GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT) {
      VUI s[GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT];
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        s[j] = vp[c + j];
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < GE_COUNT_UNROLL_CNT; ++j)
        ge_counts[c + j] = tcle::popc_mb<0>(s[j] >= vkey);
    }
    for (; c < c_end; c++) {
      ge_counts[c] = tcle::popc_mb<0>(vp[c] >= vkey);
    }
  }
  /* Scalar pad zeroing (sub0): [full_chunks, fc_al) of both count legs, so the
     EOA column loads see defined data; gt_counts is included because
     reclaim_scatter_split only refills the real chunks. The lanes
     [N, N_padded) need no separate count -- they sit in the last full chunk. */
  if (sub_idx == 0) {
    for (int c = full_chunks; c < fc_al; c++) {
      ge_counts[c] = 0u;
      gt_counts[c] = 0u;
    }
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  /* S3 stage 2 -- exclusive prefix over the chunks (sub0) and the compression
     table of the non-empty chunks (sub2), concurrently: sub0 reads ge_counts
     and writes ge_prefix, sub2 reads ge_counts and writes valid_vec_idx, so one
     barrier joins both with subs 1/3.
     The scan runs over the (matrix_col x VEC_LEN) view of the counts
     (element v = i + j*matrix_col; loadeoa/storeeoa with stride matrix_col
     move whole columns): pass 1 stores the value BEFORE adding it, so for
     matrix_col == 1 it writes zeros and lane j == counts[j] alone yields the
     exclusive prefix. row_base comes from lane_exclusive_scan over the column
     totals, because TLE-RAW has no vector arrays on the addrspace(5) stack to
     hold a running sum. Only the ge prefix is built here; the split path
     builds the gt prefix inside reclaim_scatter_split.
     n_ge / comp_cnt sit at this scope because stage 3 reads them. */
  int n_ge = 0; /* #lanes >= key */
  int comp_cnt = 0;
  int matrix_col = fc_al / VEC_LEN;
  if (sub_idx == 0) {
    VUI running_ge = (VUI)(0);
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix_col; i++) {
      VUI gv = tcle::loadeoa<VUI>(ge_counts + i, matrix_col);
      tcle::storeeoa(running_ge, ge_prefix + i, matrix_col);
      running_ge = running_ge + gv;
    }
    uint32_t acc_g = 0;
    VUI base_ge = lane_exclusive_scan(running_ge, acc_g);
    for (int i = 0; i < matrix_col; i++) {
      VUI gv = tcle::loadeoa<VUI>(ge_prefix + i, matrix_col);
      tcle::storeeoa(gv + base_ge, ge_prefix + i, matrix_col);
    }
    n_ge = static_cast<int>(acc_g); /* tail already in the counts */
    // ws[4095] is free again: desired was consumed before stage 1.
    reclaim_ws[4095] = static_cast<uint32_t>(n_ge);
  }

  /* Subthread 2 builds the compression table of the non-empty chunks: a chunk
     enters iff its ge count is nonzero (ge is a superset of gt and eq), so one
     table drives both streams and an empty chunk then costs nothing instead of
     a pair of scatter_cond. The final-pass ge band is sparse on random data, so
     comp_cnt << full_chunks. The table is the fifth leg of the layout
     (ws[4*fc_al, 5*fc_al), <= 2560 words for fc_al <= 512), i.e. it ends below
     staging. comp_cnt is published to ws[4094], read back after the barrier. */
  if (sub_idx == 2) {
    comp_cnt =
        build_valid_table(ge_counts, matrix_col, viota_base, valid_vec_idx);
    reclaim_ws[4094] = static_cast<uint32_t>(comp_cnt);
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  n_ge = static_cast<int>(reclaim_ws[4095]);
  /* comp_cnt and n_ge are read together after that barrier. sub2 never runs
     sub0's scan but must still reach the same barrier sequence (a barrier on
     only some paths aborts at runtime). */
  comp_cnt = static_cast<int>(reclaim_ws[4094]);

  /* ---- slow path decision --------------------------------------
     n_ge > K means the boundary value repeats, so the ge stream is longer
     than K and cannot be truncated by position (a late gt lane must outrank
     an early eq lane). The recount / gt-prefix / two-stream scatter then runs
     in reclaim_scatter_split(). Unique floats give n_ge == K exactly and only
     ever take the fast path below. */
  bool split_path = (n_ge > K);

  /* S3 stage 3 -- pack the selected lanes of the row into staging[0, K).
       for each chunk c of the compressed table:   // non-empty chunks
         for each lane l of vp[c]:                 // VEC_LEN sort keys
           if (vp[c][l] >= vkey) {
             stage_v[off] = value(l);              // UT2VT: key -> fp32
             stage_i[off] = c * VEC_LEN + l;       // row lane index
             off += 1;
           }
     off starts at ge_prefix[c], so chunks own disjoint slot ranges and the
     packed result keeps the row's lane order. The split path is the same
     scheme with two predicates (see the scatter helpers above).

     None of that is scalar here: off is miota(mask), the exclusive prefix of
     the compare mask, and the store is
     scatter_cond(mask, data, stage + off, rank * sizeof(float)); the metadata
     of 16 chunks arrives in one masked table load plus one gather of
     ge_prefix[c]. The table is walked in three tiers (repeat16 / repeat4 /
     remainder) so the tail never reads past comp_cnt while every tier keeps
     all subthreads busy -- comp_cnt is shared, not re-partitioned. Iterate
     [0, comp_cnt), never [0, full_chunks): valid_vec_idx only holds the
     non-empty chunks, so an index past its end is a garbage chunk id.

     Staging sits above the five count/prefix legs and below ws[4093..4095]
     (the WS_* layout at the top of the file); both streams are 512B-aligned
     because the launcher reads them as whole VEC_LEN-lane tiles. A staging
     write must never reach ge_prefix: that would mis-aim every later
     scatter. */
  float *stage_v = reinterpret_cast<float *>(reclaim_ws + WS_STAGE_OFF);
  int   *stage_i = reinterpret_cast<int   *>(reclaim_ws + WS_STAGE_I_OFF);
  if (split_path) {
    reclaim_scatter_split(su_ptr, cu16, in_kind, desired, K, matrix_col,
                          comp_cnt, sub_idx, sub_num, chunk_start, chunk_count,
                          ge_prefix, gt_counts, gt_prefix, valid_vec_idx,
                          stage_v, stage_i, &reclaim_ws[4093], viota_base);
  } else {
    int repeat16 = comp_cnt / (sub_num * 16);
    int remainder16 = comp_cnt % (sub_num * 16);
    int repeat4 = remainder16 / (sub_num * 4);
    int remainder4 = remainder16 % (sub_num * 4);
    uint32_t bpe = static_cast<uint32_t>(sizeof(uint32_t));
    for (int idx = 0; idx < repeat16; idx++) {
      int offset = idx * sub_num * 16 + sub_idx * 16;
      /* One masked 16-lane table load; padded lanes read 0. */
      VUI vec_c = tcle::load<VUI>(valid_vec_idx + offset, (VUI)(0),
                                  tcle::vset_mb<VMASK>(16));
      VUI v_ge_off = tcle::gather<VUI>(ge_prefix, vec_c * bpe);
      #pragma clang loop unroll(full)
      for (int j = 0; j < 16; ++j) {
        int c = static_cast<int>(vec_c[j]);
        VUI keys, fbits;
        chunk_vals(su_ptr, cu16, in_kind, c, keys, fbits);
        scatter_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(v_ge_off[j]),
                     viota_base, stage_v, stage_i);
      }
    }
    /* repeat4: scalar metadata reads. The 4-way loop stays a loop on purpose:
       expanding it here measured ~0.5% slower than the loop form. */
    for (int idx = 0; idx < repeat4; idx++) {
      int offset = repeat16 * sub_num * 16 + idx * sub_num * 4 + sub_idx * 4;
      for (int u = 0; u < 4; u++) {
        int c = static_cast<int>(valid_vec_idx[offset + u]);
        VUI keys, fbits;
        chunk_vals(su_ptr, cu16, in_kind, c, keys, fbits);
        scatter_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(ge_prefix[c]),
                     viota_base, stage_v, stage_i);
      }
    }
    // remainder: strided single chunks.
    for (int idx = sub_idx; idx < remainder4; idx += sub_num) {
      int offset = repeat16 * sub_num * 16 + repeat4 * sub_num * 4 + idx;
      int c = static_cast<int>(valid_vec_idx[offset]);
      VUI keys, fbits;
      chunk_vals(su_ptr, cu16, in_kind, c, keys, fbits);
      scatter_lane(keys, fbits, vkey, c, static_cast<int>(ge_prefix[c]),
                   viota_base, stage_v, stage_i);
    }
  }

  __syncsubthreads();

  // ======================================================================
  // S4  sort -- S3 left the k elements in reclaimed order, i.e. the
  //     torch.topk(sorted=False) contract. is_sorted turns that into the
  //     sorted=True contract by argsorting the staged (key, index) pairs in
  //     place; see the banner above sort_staged_pairs. It runs on subthread 0
  //     only, after the S3 barrier, so it neither races the scatters nor needs
  //     a barrier of its own.
  // ======================================================================
  if (is_sorted) {
    if (sub_idx == 0) {
      uint32_t *sv = reclaim_ws + WS_STAGE_OFF;
      uint32_t *si = reclaim_ws + WS_STAGE_I_OFF;
      int align_k =
          (static_cast<int>(K) + VEC_LEN - 1) / VEC_LEN * VEC_LEN;
      int nvec = align_k / VEC_LEN;

      /* Scratch for the radix pass (its destination must be disjoint from the
         source). Two regions are free once S4 runs:
           - ws[WS_STAGE_OFF + 2*WS_STAGE_K_MAX, WS_SIZE) is 256 words and also
             covers the three publish slots S2/S3 have finished with;
           - l1_buf is dead after S3's last read.
         The 256-word tail covers K <= VEC_LEN (one vector per stream); wider
         K uses the row window. If neither fits, the in-place bitonic network
         is still available. */
      int np2 = 1;
      while (np2 < static_cast<int>(N))
        np2 <<= 1;
      int block_n = k16 ? (np2 < VEC_LEN16 ? VEC_LEN16 : np2)
                        : (np2 < VEC_LEN ? VEC_LEN : np2);
      int l1_words = k16 ? (block_n >> 1) : block_n;
      uint32_t *scr_v = nullptr;
      uint32_t *scr_i = nullptr;
      if (align_k <= VEC_LEN) {
        scr_v = reclaim_ws + WS_STAGE_OFF + 2 * WS_STAGE_K_MAX;
        scr_i = scr_v + VEC_LEN;
      } else if (2 * align_k <= l1_words) {
        scr_v = l1_buf_;
        scr_i = l1_buf_ + align_k;
      }

      /* The radix sort handles both reclaim paths: a stable LSD pass keeps
         equal keys in staging order, and S3 pages ascending row position
         inside each group (the fast path is one group; the split path stages
         [gt][eq] and all equal keys sit inside one group), which is exactly
         the bitonic network's explicit ascending-index tie-break.
         Radix wins from K > 32: the 32-wide bitonic network is only 15
         dependent stages while the radix sort is a constant seven passes, and
         the benchmark crossover sits between (8,512,32) and (4,128,64). */
      constexpr int RADIX_SORT_MIN_K = 32;
      if (static_cast<int>(K) > RADIX_SORT_MIN_K && scr_v != nullptr) {
        sort_staged_radix(sv, si, scr_v, scr_i, static_cast<int>(K), nvec,
                          viota_base);
      } else {
        sort_staged(sv, si, static_cast<int>(K), viota_base);
      }
    }
    /* The launcher reads staging right after this call returns, and the S3
       barrier above only covers S3's scatters, so S4 needs its own: without it
       the read sees a partly updated staging window (measured: correct lanes
       0..7 of 128, the rest still holding S3's reclaimed order). */
    __syncsubthreads();
  }

  // ======================================================================
  // S5  writeback -- not in this file.
  //     stage_v / stage_i now hold the row's k elements at ws[WS_STAGE_OFF)
  //     and ws[WS_STAGE_I_OFF), values as fp32 bit patterns and indices as
  //     int32. The TLE-Struct launcher reads that K-wide tile through
  //     tle.gpu.local_ptr and stores it to output_vals/output_idx at
  //     row*K, so this region owns no DTE channel and no global store.
  //
  //     The staging window is reused by the next call, so the launcher
  //     barriers after its read; the S3 (or, for sorted calls, S4) barrier is
  //     what makes the staged pairs visible to it.
  // ======================================================================
}
